</style><Fragment set:html={siteHead()}/></head><body><Fragment set:html={siteHeader({current:'guide', lang:'en', langSwitch:false})}/><main id="content" class="guide-main"><p class="muted">EVIDENCE GUIDE · Updated October 27, 2026</p><h1>{title}</h1>
<div class="answer"><b>You usually cannot confirm AI use from a screenshot alone.</b><p>Start with a developer disclosure or a dated statement tied to the exact game. Then check which assets it covers, whether the content is still present, and what remains unverified.</p><a class="cta" href="/">Check a game →</a></div>
<nav class="steps" aria-label="Guide sections"><a href="#sources"><b>1 · Find a source</b>Prioritize original statements.</a><a href="#visual"><b>2 · Check the asset</b>Treat odd details as leads.</a><a href="#conflicts"><b>3 · Resolve conflicts</b>Compare scope and dates.</a></nav>
<section id="sources"><h2>Which evidence supports an AI-use claim?</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>What it establishes</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Developer disclosure</td><td>The developer reports the named use.</td><td>Exact wording, App ID and review date.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Developer production post or interview</td><td>An attributed account of a workflow.</td><td>Original post, named assets and later corrections.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Documented before/after asset change</td><td>An asset changed between identified versions.</td><td>Why it changed; replacement alone does not establish AI origin.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Player screenshot or allegation</td><td>A specific question to investigate.</td><td>Game version, original capture and developer response.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI-detector score or visual impression</td><td>An uncertain signal.</td><td>It cannot independently establish asset provenance.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For Steam's disclosure location, follow <a href="/guides/how-to-check-steam-game-ai/">how to see if a game uses AI on Steam</a>. Valve distinguishes content made during development from content generated while playing; those are different questions.</p></section>
<section id="visual"><h2>What if the art looks AI-generated?</h2><p>Save the exact asset and its context. Ask about provenance instead of labeling the entire game.</p>
<details><summary>Distorted anatomy, repeated shapes or unreadable lettering</summary><p>These can justify checking a source. Compression, stylized art, placeholders and ordinary mistakes can also produce unusual details. A screenshot does not identify the tool or artist.</p></details>
<details><summary>An abrupt change in art or animation style</summary><p>Compare the same asset across dated trailers, builds and developer posts. A style change alone does not distinguish AI generation from outsourced art or a different production technique.</p></details>
<details><summary>AI-sounding dialogue or voice</summary><p>Look for a statement about text-to-speech, actors or script production. A generated voice does not imply an AI-written script; a synthetic-sounding recording may have another explanation.</p></details>
<details><summary>Procedural worlds, bots or a story about AI</summary><p>Traditional procedural generation and NPC behavior do not establish generative-AI use. The subject of a story is separate from the tools used to write it.</p></details></section>
<section><h2>Keep these four conclusions separate</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Conclusion</th><th>Use it when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Disclosed use</td><td>An attributable source explicitly identifies use and scope.</td></tr><tr><td>Developer claims no use</td><td>A developer denies use within a stated scope; label it as a self-report.</td></tr><tr><td>Unverified allegation</td><td>A player raises a concern without substantiated production evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>No verified evidence found</td><td>The recorded review found no substantiated claim; this does not certify an AI-free game.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section id="conflicts"><h2>How to handle removed content and conflicting statements</h2><ol><li>Record the date and version of each statement.</li><li>Separate marketing, concept art, shipped assets and live generation.</li><li>Keep historical use separate from what is present in the current build.</li><li>Attribute a replacement claim to its source until its scope is verified.</li></ol><p>If a demo has a different disclosure, retain both App IDs. Do not transfer a demo finding to the full game automatically.</p></section>
<section><h2>Read real examples</h2><ul><li><a href="/games/2073850-the-finals/">THE FINALS</a>: a voice disclosure does not settle art or writing.</li><li><a href="/games/2456740-inzoi/">inZOI</a>: live player tools and production assets require separate findings.</li><li><a href="/games/5393000-fish-with-friends/">Fish With Friends</a>: broad asset wording leaves specific areas unspecified.</li><li><a href="/games/4019220-dressmaker/">Dressmaker</a>: distinguish a developer denial from independent certification.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2>A useful report takes one minute to structure</h2><p><b>Game + App ID · asset or feature · original source URL · exact wording · date/version · uncertainty.</b></p><p>Send a source that another person can check. Avoid guesses about intent or unsupported percentages.</p><a class="cta" href="/browse/">Find a game record and suggest a correction →</a></section></main><footer class="guide-foot">Method: <a href="/guides/how-to-check-steam-game-ai/">Steam disclosure guide</a> · Primary reference: <a href="https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey">Valve Content Survey</a>. Examples link to dated records with original sources.</footer><Fragment set:html={siteFooter({lang:'en'})}/></body></html>
